{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"**Cross-subsidization—conceptual examples.** With one portfolio, a class can bear costs or receive benefits generated by another class’s activity. For example, mutual-fund redemptions might lead the portfolio to sell securities, with trading costs and taxable gains shared across classes, including the ETF class. Conversely, ETF creations or redemptions might prompt portfolio trades or cash management that affect mutual-fund shareholders. In-kind ETF redemptions can also benefit remaining investors by removing low-basis securities without a portfolio-level sale; that benefit is not necessarily confined to ETF shareholders. These are possibilities, not automatic outcomes: effects depend on flows, basket design, portfolio activity, and expense allocation.\n\n**Representative order-specific framework.** Assess the fund’s proposed arrangements using representative ETF creation/redemption orders and related mutual-fund activity—not a universal numerical cutoff. Monitor whether costs are fairly allocated, including **transaction costs**; whether **cash** needs or residual cash create costs or distortions for either class; and whether **realized gains** or tax effects are shifted between classes. Set monitoring methods and escalation criteria appropriate to the fund, and review actual experience against the assumptions."}}
